.title = "Using sops & age to manage environment variables",
---
Managing `.env` variables without a secret management workflow gets messy, especially if you work across many projects and repositories. Once values are scattered or committed by mistake, it becomes hard to remember which `.env` belongs to which project, even with a password manager.

I prefer keeping encrypted secrets close to the code. Algorithms like AES-256 and PGP are strong in theory (see [NIST's discussion]($link.url("https://csrc.nist.gov/csrc/media/Events/2024/fifth-pqc-standardization-conference/documents/papers/on-practical-cost-of-grover.pdf").new(true))), but PGP is difficult for most people to use correctly in day-to-day workflows. If you cannot reliably decrypt your own files later, the setup is not practical.

Age is a simpler alternative. It uses modern cryptography, has manageable keys, and can work with existing credentials such as SSH keys. In this post, I use age with SOPS to encrypt secrets while keeping them in the repository.
# I am assuming that you are using some sort of \*nix based operating system if not then at least use WSL(windows subsystem for Linux) to follow along.

Here we will be using a tool like [sops(secret operations)]($link.url("https://getsops.io").new(true)) .Sops is now a CNCF sandbox project and historically it’s been a project of people working at Mozilla, and later donated the project.

Sops can manage Yaml, Toml, dotenv, env.json.

[]($image.url("https://s6.imgcdn.dev/Y8IpEH.png"))

But writing yaml files for config is something no one should be doing.

Why doing Gitops the hard way, also by not commiting enviornment variables you are not creating a reference so that others can collaborate on the project, SOPs is very selective at encryption it just encrypts the values not the keys. 

So introducing [mise]($link.url("https://mise.jdx.dev/").new(true))  my favourite tool nowadays to manage dev tools, tasks, handling environment variables, Generally it’s not recommended to pin “latest“ but i am doing so :(. 

> 💡 To generate a mise.toml run \`mise gen config\` and choose tools you need from the Tui (terminal user interface)

```yaml
[tools]
age = "latest"
sops = "latest"

[env]
SOPS_AGE_RECIPIENTS = "{your age recipients here}, {yet another recipient}"
_ = { file = "{ path = .env }" }

[tasks]
encrypt = "sops -e -i .env && mv .env .env.example"
decrypt = "mv .env.example .env && sops -d -i .env"
```

This is generally my overall workflow for secrets. The public key is safe to share.

```bash
export SOPS_AGE_KEYS="AGE_PRIVATE_KEY_HERE" 
```

or, you can use 

```sh
export SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE="$HOME/.sops/age/keys.txt"
or 
export SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE="$HOME/.config/sops/age/keys.txt"
```

keep the contents of keys.txt safe in some password manager only .  

AGE supports YubiKey, ssh-agent as a helpers too.

[asciicast]($image.url("https://asciinema.org/a/1130380.svg"))

This is the workflow with sops configuration. 

[asciicast]($image.url("https://asciinema.org/a/DByfEGsk6UQPSzmD.svg"))

Using mise makes the process straightforward. Once this is set up, you can keep encrypted secrets in the repository and decrypt them when needed.
